(window.IBALL_PERIODICAL_LIBRARY = window.IBALL_PERIODICAL_LIBRARY || {})["2026-09-07"] = {"meta":{"id":"2026-09-07","label":"2026 年 9 月 7 日 - 9 月 13 日","date":"2026-09-07","range":"2026-09-07|2026-09-13","theme":"科技","themes":["科技"],"sources":["The Guardian","BARRON’S","Economist"],"title":"AI transcriptions of talks with doctors","titleZh":"（9.7—9.13）","counts":{"article":7,"paragraph":14,"vocab":21,"question":15,"qa":1,"original":6,"layout":0},"tabs":{"articles":true,"reading":true,"tests":true,"qa":true,"originals":true}},"articles":[{"file":"9.7—9.13 原文(1).docx","articles":[{"title":"AI transcriptions of talks with doctors are ‘potential threat to safety of patients’","titleZh":"","source":"The Guardian","publishedOn":"9.7","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"AI technology that listens to and transcribes patients’ consultations with doctors can put them at risk by getting the names of drugs and illnesses wrong, an NHS watchdog has warned."},{"index":2,"text":"In one case a woman was shocked when the AI scribe’s summary of her conversation wrongly said she had demyelination – serious nerve damage that can lead to multiple sclerosis. It was only when the patient, an NHS health professional, queried the AI tool’s record of the result of her MRI scan that the hospital corrected it to what it should have been – “null demyelination”. “This was eventually corrected but it was a very traumatising experience to be given an incorrect diagnosis because of AI and then be told it’s a typo,” said the woman, who asked not to be named."},{"index":3,"text":"Healthwatch England has highlighted the case and other mistakes by AI scribes to show that the technology, which the NHS is introducing, is a potential threat to patients’ safety. Unless detected, errors could end up in patients’ medical records and affect their care, it said."},{"index":4,"text":"In another case an AI scribe confused the drug the GP had prescribed with a different one of a similar name – a blunder which, again, the patient, not the doctor, identified."},{"index":5,"text":"On another occasion an AI-generated summary letter did not say that the hospital consultant had told the patient to seek a repeat prescription from their GP for their migraine, which could have left them unable to get their medication."},{"index":6,"text":"Healthwatch, the statutory NHS patient champion, has heard “multiple stories from patients who have noticed these errors when a health professional hasn’t”, it said. “These inaccuracies may persist in records if the patient doesn’t catch them.”"},{"index":7,"text":"The government’s 10-year health plan for the NHS in England expects AI scribes to “liberate staff from their current burden of bureaucracy and administration, freeing up time to care and to focus on the patient”. It is central to the planned “big shift” from the NHS being analogue to a digital-based service."},{"index":8,"text":"Healthwatch said: “Healthcare has never been error-free. But our findings show the urgent need for clarity over how patients can report and get corrected any mistakes made by AI scribing tools or the professionals that use them.”"},{"index":9,"text":"Healthwatch added that it is “worrying” that the Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency has decided not to classify AI scribes as medical devices, which means there will be no England-wide oversight to ensure they are safe to use and effective. GPs and hospital doctors in England are already using 27 different AI scribes."},{"index":10,"text":"Ministers have been warned that the NHS and medics could be sued over mistakes made by AI."},{"index":11,"text":"AI’s accuracy was in the spotlight recently when patients in Rotherham complained to their local Healthwatch that an AI receptionist used by some local GP practices did not understand their Yorkshire accents."},{"index":12,"text":"Dr Shier Ziser Dawood, a GP in London, last year warned in a leading medical journal that AI scribes may prove “a double-edged sword” for family doctors. She recounted how an AI scribe said she had told her patient to “continue their Prozac” even though she had not prescribed or discussed that drug with them."},{"index":13,"text":"She warned that the need for doctors to review all transcripts in order to check for errors meant that AI tools are not yet proving time-saving."},{"index":14,"text":"Dr Charlotte Blease, an expert in AI use in healthcare at Uppsala university in Sweden, said “AI can and does make mistakes”, but added: “The fact is, doctors can and do make mistakes without AI. And it is certainly possible the error rate is worse.”"}]},{"title":"Changes to vet practice rules ‘put pet owners at risk of being ripped off ’","titleZh":"","source":"The Guardian","publishedOn":"9.8","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"Pet owners have been warned they risk being ripped off by private equity firms that have bought local veterinary clinics after the Competition and Markets Authority (CMA) removed requirements for multinational companies to disclose which practices they owned."},{"index":2,"text":"The CMA recently ran an investigation into the monopolisation of vet practices and found the lack of competition and transparency of ownership had led to high prices and a lack of information for customers. Pet owners were often unaware their local vet practice could be owned by an international private equity company."},{"index":3,"text":"It found pet owners paid 16.6% more on average at large vet groups than at independent practices. It said the £6.3bn market was not fit for purpose and needed to be modernised."},{"index":4,"text":"As a result, the CMA and ministers announced proposals in a white paper earlier this summer including the capping of prescriptions for pets at £21 and more transparency over ownership."},{"index":5,"text":"But the professional body the Progressive Veterinary Association (PVA) has threatened the government agency with a judicial review after it changed the wording in its plans in a way the PVA says allows multinational companies to obscure their ownership of vet practices."},{"index":6,"text":"More than 60% of veterinary practices are owned in whole or in part by six groups: CVS, Pets at Home, Medivet, IVC and VetPartners, which are owned by private equity investors; and Linnaeus, whose parent company is Mars Petcare, a subsidiary of the US confectionery group Mars."},{"index":7,"text":"Rather than requiring the name of large multinational companies or private equity investors to be listed as the owner of the vet practice, the new rules would allow corporates to use a brand name or the name of the original independent practice."},{"index":8,"text":"The initial wording of the proposal from the CMA said vets had to disclose their ownership and name the “corporate vet group”. This has been softened to “network or group”, meaning the name of the vet practice or subsidiary of the company could be named instead of the conglomerate."},{"index":9,"text":"Dr Iain McGill, a director of the PVA, told the Guardian: “This is bad news for pet owners and their animals. Ultimately, large corporations would be allowed to hide the fact that they are the ultimate controller of local vet practices and operate behind sometimes misleading brand names.”"},{"index":10,"text":"He added: “Where they have a choice, pet owners often prefer independent practices, which the CMA found on average to be cheaper than corporate practices.”"},{"index":11,"text":"The CMA told vets who complained that the disclosure of the ultimate parent company names might not be of any meaningful benefit to pet owners. It added that these corporate names could be unrecognisable and that pet owners were more familiar with the high street vet or pet company names."},{"index":12,"text":"However, the PVA argued that pet owners had the right to know if their local vet practice was owned by a large conglomerate."},{"index":13,"text":"A CMA spokesperson said: “It is critical that pet owners know whether their local practice is part of a national group or locally owned, and we are confident the changes we are making will achieve this."},{"index":14,"text":"“In future, all practices will have to make ownership links clear by using brand names that people recognise on signs and online."},{"index":15,"text":"“Our proposals were supported by large and small vet practices and consumer groups, and we made our decision on this in our final report in March."},{"index":16,"text":"“We’re now considering comments on exactly how the changes should be put in place to make sure they are clear and workable for pet owners and vets.”"}]},{"title":"As Billionaires’ Ranks Swell, Here’s One Living His Best Life","titleZh":"","source":"BARRON’S","publishedOn":"9.9","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"How was your August? For aviation mogul Kenn Ricci, the answer must be fantastico, after his epic 70th birthday party on Italy’s Lake Como. The multi-night, black-tie affair, which per Italian media featured entertainment from Mariah Carey, John Legend, Elton John, and Kenny G, was held at the Villa d’Este in Cernobbio."},{"index":2,"text":"The five-star luxury hotel, formerly a noble residence from the 16th century, is a “favorite and regular stop for Ricci [pronounced ‘Ricky’]… on his many visits to Lake Como for over 30 years,” notes the website of Flexjet, the company he chairs."},{"index":3,"text":"The party had locals wondering whether hosting an affair of this magnitude was too much for Lake Como. Leading up to the party, news outlet La Provencia reported that a “small army is hard at work: hundreds of professionals including artistic directors, audio/lighting technicians, set designers, drivers, makeup artists, hairstylists, and service staff. Other villas and lodging facilities have been reserved to accommodate the crew.”"},{"index":4,"text":"It’s the stuff of “Champagne wishes and caviar dreams,” as Robin Leach used to say—and some light summer fare for Barron’s readers. But Ricci’s blowout is also emblematic of the flood of wealthy Americans invading Europe (basking in the glow of the strong U.S. dollar), and the soaring business of private aviation (no doubt, many of his guests flew on Ricci’s Flexjet—a smaller, privately held competitor of Berkshire Hathaway–owned NetJets)."},{"index":5,"text":"Most significantly, though, Ricci’s soiree reflects the unfettered, inexorable rise of the ultra rich, a point brought home in a new report, Billionaire Census 2026, by research firm Altrata. The study calculated that the number of billionaires around the globe climbed 8.2% in 2025 to 3,795, the biggest increase in five years. As for their wealth, that was up a brisk 12.8% to a record $15.1 trillion, equal to 20% of the market cap of the S&amp;P 500 index, the third consecutive year of accelerating growth."},{"index":6,"text":"The main driver, of course, was the U.S. tech business. Ball don’t lie. The number of billionaires in the U.S., home to a third of the world’s “billies,” grew 11.5%, to 1,265. They’re worth $6.7 trillion, or 44% of global billionaire wealth. Yes, China’s billionaire population climbed 13%, but the U.S. still has three times more."},{"index":7,"text":"Then there’s the very top of the food chain. “The planet’s 29 richest people, each with fortunes above $50 billion, had a combined net worth of $4.1 trillion, or a staggering 27% of all billionaire wealth,” Altrata notes."},{"index":8,"text":"This domain is dominated by U.S. tech bros. According to the Bloomberg Billionaire List, the nine richest people on the planet are all U.S. technology male centibillionaires, worth collectively over $2.6 trillion."},{"index":9,"text":"As for metropolises, don’t tell Zohran Mamdani, but New York is the leading city for billionaires, with that population rising 8% last year to 164. New York has more billionaires than any country other than Germany, China, and of course, the U.S. San Francisco comes in at No.3, with 99."},{"index":10,"text":"The No.2 and No.4 cities, Hong Kong and London, respectively, were the only top 15 municipalities whose billionaire population fell. One could argue both are retreating from globalism—in London’s case, post-Brexit, and in Hong Kong’s, the Chinese government’s economic and societal crackdown. Singapore’s billionaire population grew the most last year, perhaps at Hong Kong’s expense."},{"index":11,"text":"But back to the pride of Cleveland, Ricci, who didn’t make his fortune via today’s typical wealth-creating acronyms (GPUs or AI). Ricci got super rich the old-fashioned way, he earned it (as John Houseman said in Smith Barney ads of yore), serving the super rich; specifically, selling them fractional shares of private jets. Flexjet doesn’t release financials beyond saying it did $4.6 billion in combined 2025 year-end revenue—up from zero when it was founded in 1995. Last year, private-equity group L Catterton, which is backed by LVMH, led an $800 million investment in Flexjet."},{"index":12,"text":"Ricci is a Horatio Alger story on wings. Growing up in modest means and beginning as a pilot out of the Air Force, Ricci borrowed money, including $500 from his dad, and bought a small charter business. He flew musical acts like Barbra Streisand and Elton John, and became Bill Clinton’s pilot on his run for president in 1992. Ricci would later partner with Raytheon Technologies (now RTX), and then buy Flexjet from Bombardier."},{"index":13,"text":"“We’ve chosen as a family not to disguise and not to hide from our wealth,” he said recently in “How Kenn Ricci Went From Pilot to Billionaire,” the first episode of The WSJ Money Interview. “We have family meetings every quarter, and we’re passing on wealth. I’m not waiting till I die. I want to watch my kids enjoy their wealth. We have a family limited partnership the kids can borrow from to start businesses.” August 31, 2026 | BARRON’S"}]},{"title":"Anti-social","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.10","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"It seemed A parent’s dream. To manage the time her 14‑year‑old son spent online, Harini Naidu had tried various tactics: from restricting Wi‑Fi access to turning the internet off at night. So she was excited when Australia became the first country in the world to ban social‑media accounts for under‑16s. But the rules, imposed in December, “literally did nothing”. Her son lost his YouTube account, but can still watch videos without logging in. Ms Naidu’s daughter Ishana was 15, but has kept her accounts on X, Instagram, Snapchat and TikTok. Ishana says: “It’s so underwhelming that people have forgotten there’s a ban.” They do not even have to hide behind virtual private networks."},{"index":2,"text":"The experiences of teens like Ishana matter to other countries, too. Since Australia brought in its curbs, dozens have done the same, or are considering it. On August 24th New Zealand announced a planned ban. Policymakers worry that social media cause various harms to the young. They also know that bans are popular with adults. Many cheered on August 26th when Meta agreed to change its products for under‑18s in most American states, including a two‑hours‑per‑day limit on their scrolling, after settling a lawsuit alleging its platforms harm youngsters."},{"index":3,"text":"Yet so far, Australia’s policy has proved largely ineffective. At first the government was able to tout some successes. Social‑media platforms had removed, restricted or deactivated almost 5m accounts in the first month. And it may be too early to write off the effectiveness of Australia’s new rules. But studies have shown they have so far had minimal impact on usage. One in the British Medical Journal in June found that, three months after restrictions were introduced, 85% of under‑16s said they still used banned platforms. Twelve‑ and 13‑year‑olds spent the same amount of time on social media as before the ban. Worries that children migrate to alternative platforms, with lower safety standards, were mostly unfounded—but largely because so many remain on existing ones."},{"index":4,"text":"Young Aussies are keen to stay online. Some spy a challenge, says Ishana, as under‑16s “want to use social media more because it’s banned”. The young “know how to outsmart the system”, she explains, including by creating joint accounts with friends, which she thinks confuses age‑checking systems. Yet she says several accounts with her real date of birth were not removed. Many under‑16s have made new accounts using a fake age. Others have entered their parents’ or siblings’ details."},{"index":5,"text":"Some blame tech firms for not enforcing the rules. KJR, a software‑testing company, recently opened 50 social‑media accounts in Australia claiming the user to be 16. It said only one site did any verification and all 50 accounts remained active. The government is trying to beef up its rules, including by doubling fines on offending firms to A$99m ($71m). It also wants to give Australia’s eSafety Commissioner power to make social‑media firms hand over internal‑usage data."},{"index":6,"text":"Others blame the policy’s ineffectiveness so far partly on the technology meant to enforce it. Firms are using various tools, including AI, to analyse posts and scan faces. But facial‑age estimation software is not very accurate in distinguishing between, say, a 15‑ and 16‑year‑old. Lucina, a 15‑year‑old from the state of Victoria, says her face was scanned on Snapchat and she was told she looked 18. “The policy hasn’t been tested because it’s been technically impossible to enforce,” says Tama Leaver of Curtin University in Perth."},{"index":7,"text":"Mr Leaver thinks other countries planning similar laws are concluding that the “only way to do this is to require people’s real IDs”—ie, an official digital or physical identity card—when they sign up to social media. Malaysia, one of just a few other countries where Australian‑style restrictions are in force, has opted for this method. L.S. Leonard, the chief legal counsel of Malaysia’s Communications and Multimedia Commission, the regulator, says that its assessment indicated that this would be “much more accurate” than the age‑assurance approach used in Australia."},{"index":8,"text":"Yet things are not going to plan in Malaysia either. Mr Leonard says social‑media platforms claim to have removed lots of under‑age accounts, according to their own guidelines. But nothing has changed for most users, says Tricia Yeoh of the University of Nottingham’s Malaysian branch. Mr Leonard says platforms have asked for time to “make their system adaptable” for age verification; he expects everything “to be resolved” by January, after which non‑compliers may be fined. Ms Yeoh, however, suspects platforms are worried about the risks of storing personal IDs on systems that could be hacked, or subject to requests from governments."},{"index":9,"text":"Some oppose blunt social‑media bans like Australia’s because they think they let platforms get away with not making their sites safe. Ways to incentivise firms to do so are being contemplated. Canada plans to ban platforms from allowing under‑16s to have social‑media accounts, but only until these apps meet a set of safety criteria. And there are hopes that Meta, after introducing limits for teens in most American states, will roll the same features out elsewhere, and that other social‑media firms will follow suit."},{"index":10,"text":"Young Australians think they deserve more credit: Ms Naidu’s 14‑year‑old son says many teenagers already “know how to be safe online”. He stays off X, for example, because it is “very unfiltered”. He prefers Reddit, which is “less unhinged”. September 5TH-11TH ｜Asia"}]},{"title":"Patronise me. Please","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.11","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"One name gets top billing at the Bayeux tapestry show at the British Museum, and it’s not William the Conqueror. It is Igor Tulchinsky, the boss of WorldQuant, an asset‑management firm. He is the only listed sponsor, so the blockbuster show is “presented by” him. He reportedly stumped up £5m ($6.8m)."},{"index":2,"text":"In Britain individual patrons are in. At museums and art galleries, their names are increasingly visible. Whereas high culture in continental Europe is still largely state‑funded, Britain is moving closer to the American model of private support for creators and curators."},{"index":3,"text":"Patrons’ rise partly reflects a squeeze on public funding. Tate, a group of galleries, received £56m in 2009‑10 and £54m in 2024‑25: a fall of about a third in real terms. The British government urges museums to foster a “culture of giving”, like their American peers. Museums—particularly those with free admission—have had to up their fundraising game."},{"index":4,"text":"Individual donors are also, to some extent, replacing corporate sponsors. Research by Arts and Business, a charity, found that corporate backing for culture in Britain fell for four straight years after 2007, when the global financial crisis began. And as anti‑corporate protests grow bolder, such sponsorship is more likely to bring bad publicity for donor and recipient alike. BP, an oil firm, was once a prominent sponsor of culture in Britain. But museums and galleries, being open to the public, are soft targets for activists, who have tarred and feathered the entrance to the Tate (using a non‑toxic tar substitute) and posted a spoof exhibit at the British Museum on plans to drill for oil under Stonehenge."},{"index":5,"text":"All of which has made private patrons more appealing. Big institutions have brought in specialists to woo them. In 2024‑25, 15 national museums in Britain brought in £1.2bn through fundraising—three times their haul in 2023‑24. Most are screening donors more carefully, too. The National Gallery has a committee to investigate “ethical and reputational issues” associated with gifts."},{"index":6,"text":"Patrons enjoy all kinds of benefits. Their donations can sometimes attract tax breaks. Being associated with a prestigious museum confers status, which some rich people apparently crave. And many donors simply love the arts and are happy to bankroll them."},{"index":7,"text":"Many are eager to support specific causes they hold dear, says Clarissa Levi, an art lawyer. Mr Tulchinsky has said that the “symmetries and proportions” in the Bayeux tapestry appeal to his quantitative sensibilities. However, he was impelled to support the exhibition by his belief in the importance of education, shaped by his own experience of moving to America from Soviet Belarus as a child. His donation will pay for learning materials for schools in the hope that “bringing young people face‑to‑face with this extraordinary artefact will create a lasting impact.”"},{"index":8,"text":"There can be drawbacks to relying on philanthropists. Being human, they may be fickle or bossy. The National Gallery says it relies on private support to help fund “several senior curatorial posts”, among other things. Pushy billionaire donors might withdraw support for curators they do not like or try to have shows they disagree with axed."},{"index":9,"text":"The history of art is full of overweening patrons. Michelangelo had to deal with one who, being pope, thought he was infallible, which was annoying. But the great advantage of rich individuals is that, by contrast with governments, there are plenty to choose from. September 5TH-11TH ｜Culture"}]},{"title":"Well Informed","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.12","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"Exercising can be tedious. Video games are fun. What if you could combine them? Playing games while wearing a virtual-reality (VR) headset, with a motion-sensitive controller in each hand, can be taxing. “Pistol Whip” makes you step from side to side and squat frequently as you gun down foes and dodge bullets. “The Thrill of the Fight” is a virtual boxing game that works up a real sweat. In “Supernatural” and “Beat Saber” you punch and slice moving targets in time to pumping music. But does any of this count as proper exercise?"},{"index":2,"text":"The answer is yes, though with caveats. One way to evaluate VR exercise is a measure called metabolic equivalent of task (MET). Someone at rest expends about one MET. Walking is three METs. Playing football, ten. Anything over six METs is considered “vigorous” exercise. Researchers at San Francisco State University determined that playing “The Thrill of the Fight” (which is, in fact, exhausting) was a 9.3 MET activity. Other work, published in JMIR Serious Games, found the “Flow” and “Boxing” modes in “Supernatural” also counted as vigorous exercise, at around eight METs."},{"index":3,"text":"In yet another study, published in Games for Health Journal in 2024, participants played a sedentary video game, two VR games, and walked on a treadmill, each for ten minutes, while having their oxygen consumption measured. Playing “The Thrill of the Fight” was comparable in aerobic intensity to walking on a treadmill, and raised heart rates by a similar amount."},{"index":4,"text":"So much for cardio. What about weight loss? Work published last year in Nature Medicine divided 227 adolescents into five groups: real table tennis, real football, virtual table tennis, virtual football and a control group. There was no significant difference in weight loss between the real and virtual groups. Members of both lost about 5kg after eight weeks. Those in the control group lost none."},{"index":5,"text":"But not all VR games are created equal. The variation in intensity between different games is greater than that between real and virtual exercise. The 2024 study, for example, found that “Beat Saber” counted as only light exercise (2-3 METs). And even vigorous games such as “The Thrill of the Fight” involve only arm movements and squats. Virtual objects cannot put up any real-world resistance, so VR games are no good for strength training."},{"index":6,"text":"Researchers at the University of California, Los Angeles, got round this by augmenting a cable-resistance training machine with a VR headset running a tower-defence game (in which the player must protect a stronghold from waves of virtual enemies), so that pulldowns, deadlifts and other moves triggered different attacks. Two groups trained for four weeks, one with the VR add-on, the other without. Members of the VR group showed greater improvement in strength, endurance and peak leg power. And they perceived their exertion level to be lower than it actually was."},{"index":7,"text":"Several other projects have found the same thing. People exercising in VR consistently underestimate how hard they are working. They also find it more fun than conventional exercise. But does this make them more likely to keep doing it? The evidence is unclear. Beyond this research, VR-exercise studies are often small and do not use comparisons or controls that allow their effects to be assessed. All that said, you don’t need a trial to show that VR exercise is better than none. September 5TH-11TH ｜Science & technology"}]},{"title":"Recipe for trouble","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.13","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"Pigs were once the biggest consumers of whey, a byproduct of cheesemaking. Now farmers can scarcely afford it. Demand for whey concentrate, a common ingredient in gym shakes, has soared amid the craze for consuming more protein. Dairies have not scaled up cheese production to match. The price of whey protein thus rose fivefold in the three years to June, according to Vesper, a research firm. The great comeback of cottage cheese, which TikTok influencers also recommend for bulking up, has seen a similar shortage."},{"index":2,"text":"Both cases illustrate a new problem for the food-and-drinks industry. Online trends can build demand for ingredients faster than ever before, but farms and factories cannot scale production at the same speed. Firms are adapting by learning to anticipate trends and ride them out before consumers move on."},{"index":3,"text":"Food fads are hardly new. Pineapples were so coveted in Georgian England that rental shops opened peddling display fruits. Swiss fondue, a hit with Americans in the 1960s and 70s, resulted in millions of cheese-melting pots that now languish in cupboards. Today, though, social media spreads such trends across the world in weeks. When a video of a pistachio-pastry chocolate bar made in Dubai went viral on TikTok in 2023, it was viewed more than 100m times. Months later top chocolatiers were selling their own versions, straining global pistachio production."},{"index":4,"text":"Food and drinks are especially prone to going viral. Grass-green matcha or violet ube lattes make for pleasing Instagram snaps. They are also cheap enough to be accessible. Bought once, even a $19 strawberry from Japan won’t break the bank. Young buyers, who spend a good deal of their lives on social media, are also more inclined towards novelty, notes Dan Frommer, author of “The New Consumer”, a blog."},{"index":5,"text":"Explosive demand has overwhelmed supply chains. Tencha leaves, which are used to make matcha, nearly tripled in price between 2024 and 2025 amid surging interest in the drink. The bushes from which they come require years to grow. Hot markets also attract chancers. Rocky Xu, a matcha seller in Los Angeles, says that when he ran out last year, he found that the market had become crowded with middlemen. The newcomers—mainly Japanese entrepreneurs hoping to mediate between farmers and foreign buyers—pushed up prices."},{"index":6,"text":"Food companies are trying to cope in several ways. Anticipating trends as early as possible allows them to lock in suppliers before rivals do. Starbucks, an American coffee chain, started offering matcha drinks in 2006, long before the matcha boom in the West. It popularised drinks with “popping pearls” in 2024, before bubble tea went mainstream. More recently it added a protein range to its coffees, which are now as popular as flat whites in Europe. Louise Direito, an executive at the chain, says that it has switched from relying on “small focus groups” to tracking “millions of customers shaping trends in real time” on the internet. “The challenge is knowing when to join the conversation, as well as when it’s too late,” she adds."},{"index":7,"text":"Collaborations also help. As spicy instant ramyeon noodles became all the rage in 2022, fast-food chains lined up to work with Fly by Jing, a trendy maker of chilli-oil. Shake Shack, a burger chain, built a whole chicken menu around it."},{"index":8,"text":"But not all fads are worth embracing. Starbucks’ olive-oil coffees were pulled from menus in America and Canada less than a year after their launch in 2024—amid complaints about laxative effects. September 5TH-11TH ｜Business"}]}]}],"reading":[{"file":"9.7 精读(1).pdf","headline":{"title":"AI transcriptions of talks with doctors are","titleZh":"","source":"The Guardian","series":"考研那些事儿之科技系列"},"paragraphs":[{"index":1,"en":"AI technology that listens to and transcribes patients’ consultations with doctors can put them at risk by getting the names of drugs and illnesses wrong, an NHS watchdog has warned.","zh":"英国国民保健制度（NHS）监察机构发出警告：用于收听并转录医患问诊对话的人工智能技术，可能会错误识别药物名称与病症，使患者面临风险。"},{"index":2,"en":"In one case a woman was shocked when the AI scribe’s summary of her conversation wrongly said she had demyelination – serious nerve damage that can lead to multiple sclerosis. It was only when the patient, an NHS health professional, queried the AI tool’s record of the result of her MRI scan that the hospital corrected it to what it should have been – “null demyelination”. “This was eventually corrected but it was a very traumatising experience to be given an incorrect diagnosis because of AI and then be told it’s a typo,” said the woman, who asked not to be named.","zh":"有这样一则案例：一名女性看到人工智能记录助手对其问诊对话的摘要震惊不已，摘要错误地显示她患有脱髓鞘病变——一种可诱发多发性硬化症的严重神经损伤。该患者本身是英国国民保健制度（NHS）的医护人员，正是由于她对人工智能工具记录的核磁共振扫描结果提出质疑，医院才将记录更正为正确表述：无脱髓鞘改变。这位要求匿名的女性表示：“虽然错误最终得到纠正，但因人工智能给出错误诊断，之后又被告知只是录入笔误，这段经历给我造成了极大的心理创伤。”"},{"index":3,"en":"Healthwatch England has highlighted the case and other mistakes by AI scribes to show that the technology, which the NHS is introducing, is a potential threat to patients’ safety. Unless detected, errors could end up in patients’ medical records and affect their care, it said.","zh":"英国健康监督局强调了该案例以及人工智能问诊记录助手出现的其他失误，以此警示：英国国民保健制度（NHS）正在推行的这项技术，会对患者安全构成潜在威胁。该局表示，倘若这些错误未被发现，就会留存到患者病历当中，进而影响诊疗工作。"},{"index":4,"en":"In another case an AI scribe confused the drug the GP had prescribed with a different one of a similar name – a blunder which, again, the patient, not the doctor, identified.","zh":"另有一起案例：人工智能问诊记录助手把全科医生开具的药物，错识别成另一种名称相近的药品。这一重大失误依旧是由患者本人发现，而非接诊医生。"},{"index":5,"en":"On another occasion an AI-generated summary letter did not say that the hospital consultant had told the patient to seek a repeat prescription from their GP for their migraine, which could have left them unable to get their medication.","zh":"还有一例：人工智能生成的摘要信函，未记录医院专科医生曾叮嘱患者，要找全科医生续开偏头痛药物处方。该疏漏本有可能导致患者无法拿到所需药物。"},{"index":6,"en":"Healthwatch, the statutory NHS patient champion, has heard “multiple stories from patients who have noticed these errors when a health professional hasn’t”, it said. “These inaccuracies may persist in records if the patient doesn’t catch them.”","zh":"健康监督局是英国法定的NHS患者权益机构，该机构表示：“已有多名患者反映，医护人员未能发现AI笔录错误，反而是患者本人察觉到了问题。倘若患者没有发现，错误信息就会一直留存于病历之中。”"},{"index":7,"en":"The government’s 10-year health plan for the NHS in England expects AI scribes to “liberate staff from their current burden of bureaucracy and administration, freeing up time to care and to focus on the patient”. It is central to the planned “big shift” from the NHS being analogue to a digitalbased service.","zh":"英国政府针对英格兰地区国民保健制度（NHS）出台的十年医疗规划提出，人工智能问诊笔录工具有望“把医护人员从当下繁重的行政事务中解放出来，腾出更多时间开展医疗照护、专注服务患者”。NHS旨在实现从传统模式向数字化服务的重大转型，而AI记录助手正是这场转型的核心。"},{"index":8,"en":"Healthwatch said: “Healthcare has never been error-free. But our findings show the urgent need for clarity over how patients can report and get corrected any mistakes made by AI scribing tools or the professionals that use them.”","zh":"英国健康监督局表示：“医疗行业从来都无法做到零差错。但我们的调查结果表明，当下亟需明确流程，让患者知道如何上报由AI记录助手或其使用者医务人员造成的错误，并对这些错误加以纠正。”"},{"index":9,"en":"Healthwatch added that it is “worrying” that the Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency has decided not to classify AI scribes as medical devices, which means there will be no England-wide oversight to ensure they are safe to use and effective. GPs and hospital doctors in England are already using 27 different AI scribes.","zh":"英国健康监督局还称，药品与医疗保健产品监管局决定不将人工智能问诊记录助手划归为医疗器械，这一情况“令人担忧”。这意味着英格兰地区不会建立统一的机制来保障这类工具使用安全且有效。目前英格兰的全科医生与医院医师已经在使用27种不同的AI问诊记录助手。"},{"index":10,"en":"Ministers have been warned that the NHS and medics could be sued over mistakes made by AI.","zh":"政府大臣已收到警告：国民保健制度以及医护人员有可能因人工智能造成的失误而遭到起诉。"},{"index":11,"en":"AI’s accuracy was in the spotlight recently when patients in Rotherham complained to their local Healthwatch that an AI receptionist used by some local GP practices did not understand their Yorkshire accents.","zh":"人工智能的准确度近来备受关注：罗瑟勒姆地区的患者向当地健康监督局投诉，当地部分全科诊所使用的人工智能接待系统无法识别他们的约克郡口音。"},{"index":12,"en":"Dr Shier Ziser Dawood, a GP in London, last year warned in a leading medical journal that AI scribes may prove “a double-edged sword” for family doctors. She recounted how an AI scribe said she had told her patient to “continue their Prozac” even though she had not prescribed or discussed that drug with them.","zh":"伦敦全科医生希尔・齐泽・达乌德博士去年在一本权威医学期刊中警告：人工智能问诊记录助手对家庭医生而言或许是一把“双刃剑”。她讲述了一则经历：AI笔录系统记录显示，她曾告知患者“继续服用百忧解”，但实际上她既没有给该患者开具这款药物，也从未与其讨论过此药。"},{"index":13,"en":"She warned that the need for doctors to review all transcripts in order to check for errors meant that AI tools are not yet proving time-saving.","zh":"她警示道，医生必须审阅全部转录文稿以核查错误，这意味着人工智能工具目前尚未证明能够节省时间。"},{"index":14,"en":"Dr Charlotte Blease, an expert in AI use in healthcare at Uppsala university in Sweden, said “AI can and does make mistakes”, but added: “The fact is, doctors can and do make mistakes without AI. And it is certainly possible the error rate is worse.”","zh":"瑞典乌普萨拉大学医疗人工智能领域专家夏洛特・布利斯博士表示：“人工智能会犯错，也确实犯过错。”但她补充道：“事实是，即便没有人工智能，医生同样也会出错。当然，AI工具的出错率完全有可能更高。”"}],"vocab":[{"term":"transcribe","head":"v. SYN: copy, record Researchers transcribe interview recordings into texts for qualitative analysis.","definition":"SYN: copy, record Researchers transcribe interview recordings into texts for qualitative analysis.","gloss":"转录；把语音转写成文字；抄写研究人员将访谈录音转写成文本，用于定性分析。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"consultation","head":"n. SYN: discussion, negotiation Before making major decisions, adequate consultation with relevant stakeholders is essential.","definition":"SYN: discussion, negotiation Before making major decisions, adequate consultation with relevant stakeholders is essential.","gloss":"咨询；问诊；磋商 【考研大纲词汇】作出重大决策之前，与相关利益方充分磋商十分必要。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研 大纲词汇"]},{"term":"watchdog","head":"n. SYN: discussion, negotiation Before making major decisions, adequate consultation with relevant stakeholders is essential.","definition":"SYN: discussion, negotiation Before making major decisions, adequate consultation with relevant stakeholders is essential.","gloss":"咨询；问诊；磋商 【考研大纲词汇】作出重大决策之前，与相关利益方充分磋商十分必要。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲 词汇"]},{"term":"scribe","head":"n. SYN: recorder In ancient times, the scribe was responsible for copying important documents manually.","definition":"SYN: recorder In ancient times, the scribe was responsible for copying important documents manually.","gloss":"抄写员；记录助手在古代，抄写员负责手工誊写重要文书。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"demyelination","head":"n. Demyelination is a kind of pathological change related to the human nervous system.","definition":"Demyelination is a kind of pathological change related to the human nervous system.","gloss":"脱髓鞘（病变）脱髓鞘是一种和人体神经系统相关的病理改变。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"sclerosis","head":"n. Multiple sclerosis is a chronic disease that damages the human nervous system.","definition":"Multiple sclerosis is a chronic disease that damages the human nervous system.","gloss":"硬化症；硬化多发性硬化症是一种损伤人体神经系统的慢性病。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"traumatising","head":"a. dj. SYN: distressing","definition":"dj. SYN: distressing","gloss":"造成心理创伤的","example":"Witnessing violent incidents can be a deeply traumatising experience for teenagers.","exampleZh":"目睹暴力事件对于青少年而言会是一段造成严重心理创伤的经历。","tags":[]},{"term":"typo","head":"n. SYN: misprint Careful proofreading can effectively avoid typos in academic papers.","definition":"SYN: misprint Careful proofreading can effectively avoid typos in academic papers.","gloss":"打字错误；录入笔误仔细校对可以有效避免学术论文当中的录入笔误。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"prescribe","head":"v. SYN: stipulate The rules prescribe that all participants must abide by the unified competition standards.","definition":"SYN: stipulate The rules prescribe that all participants must abide by the unified competition standards.","gloss":"开（药方）；规定，指定 【考研大纲词汇】规则规定所有参赛者都必须遵守统一的竞赛标准。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研 大纲词汇"]},{"term":"blunder","head":"n. SYN: mistake, fault A strategic blunder may bring irreversible losses to the development of an enterprise.","definition":"SYN: mistake, fault A strategic blunder may bring irreversible losses to the development of an enterprise.","gloss":"重大失误，荒唐错误 【考研大纲词汇】一次战略上的重大失误，可能会给企业发展带来不可挽回的损失。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲 词汇"]},{"term":"migraine","head":"n. SYN: headache Long-term mental stress and lack of sleep may trigger frequent migraine.","definition":"SYN: headache Long-term mental stress and lack of sleep may trigger frequent migraine.","gloss":"偏头痛长期精神压力与睡眠不足可能诱发频繁的偏头痛。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"statutory","head":"a. dj. SYN: legal, official","definition":"dj. SYN: legal, official","gloss":"法定的，依照法律规定的","example":"Citizens enjoy statutory rights and shall also perform corresponding legal obligations.","exampleZh":"公民享有法定权利，同时也应当履行相应的法律义务。","tags":[]},{"term":"persist","head":"v. SYN: continue, endure If poverty persists in some regions, more targeted support policies should be carried out.","definition":"SYN: continue, endure If poverty persists in some regions, more targeted support policies should be carried out.","gloss":"持续存在；执意，坚持 【考研大纲词汇】倘若部分地区贫困问题持续存在，就应当推行更有针对性的扶持政策。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词 汇"]},{"term":"liberate","head":"v. SYN: free, release Advanced automated equipment can liberate workers from heavy manual labor.","definition":"SYN: free, release Advanced automated equipment can liberate workers from heavy manual labor.","gloss":"解放；使摆脱束缚 【考研大纲词汇】先进自动化设备能够将工人从繁重的体力劳动中解放出来。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词 汇"]},{"term":"bureaucracy","head":"n. SYN: red-tape Excessive bureaucracy will reduce the operating efficiency of public institutions.","definition":"SYN: red-tape Excessive bureaucracy will reduce the operating efficiency of public institutions.","gloss":"官僚体系；官僚作风；行政文书事务过度的官僚作风会降低公共机构的运行效率。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"analogue","head":"n. SYN: counterpart Many traditional analogue devices are gradually replaced by digital intelligent products.","definition":"SYN: counterpart Many traditional analogue devices are gradually replaced by digital intelligent products.","gloss":"模拟事物；对应物 【考研大纲词汇】许多传统模拟设备正逐步被数字化智能产品取代。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词 汇"]},{"term":"sue","head":"v. SYN: prosecute Consumers have the right to sue enterprises for losses caused by defective products.","definition":"SYN: prosecute Consumers have the right to sue enterprises for losses caused by defective products.","gloss":"起诉，控告消费者有权就缺陷产品造成的损失起诉企业。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"spotlight","head":"n. SYN:focus, highlight The severe environmental problem has come into the spotlight amid rapid urban‑ economic development.","definition":"SYN:focus, highlight The severe environmental problem has come into the spotlight amid rapid urban‑ economic development.","gloss":"公众关注的焦点；聚光灯【考研大纲词汇】在城市经济快速发展的背景下，这一严峻环境问题已成为公众关注的焦点。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大 纲词汇"]},{"term":"receptionist","head":"n. SYN: clerk The receptionist is responsible for receiving visitors and sorting daily mails in the office building.","definition":"SYN: clerk The receptionist is responsible for receiving visitors and sorting daily mails in the office building.","gloss":"接待员；前台人员前台接待员负责在办公楼接待访客并整理日常邮件。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"a double-edged sword","head":"n. SYN: two-sided coin Technological innovation is a double‑ edged sword: it boosts productivity while bringing new social risks.","definition":"SYN: two-sided coin Technological innovation is a double‑ edged sword: it boosts productivity while bringing new social risks.","gloss":"双刃剑；利弊并存的事物技术创新是一把双刃剑，它提升生产力的同时也带来新的社会风险。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"recount","head":"v. SYN: narrate, describe The old man likes to recount his youthful struggles to the younger generation.","definition":"SYN: narrate, describe The old man likes to recount his youthful struggles to the younger generation.","gloss":"叙述，讲述（经历、事件） 【考研大纲词汇】这位老人喜欢向晚辈讲述自己年轻时艰苦奋斗的经历。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研 大纲词汇"]}]}],"tests":[{"file":"【福利】9.7 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","type":"reading","instructions":[],"passage":[],"items":[{"number":21,"stem":"According to Paragraph 1, the NHS watchdog warns that AI consultation scribes may _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"reduce bureaucratic work by automatically correcting medical records"},{"key":"B","text":"endanger patients by getting the names of medicines and illnesses wrong"},{"key":"C","text":"replace doctors' clinical judgements in diagnosing complex nerve damage"},{"key":"D","text":"fail only when patients speak with strong regional accents"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"[B]","analysis":["【题型】细节理解题·首段观点识别","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1","【解析】","- 原文（Para.1）：AI technology that listens to and transcribes patients' consultations with doctors can put them at risk by getting the names of drugs and illnesses wrong, an NHS watchdog has warned.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：偷换概念陷阱。减少行政负担是后文政府计划的目标，不是 watchdog 在首段发出的安全警告。","- 选项B（正确）：首段明确说该技术会因弄错药品和疾病名称而使患者处于风险中。B 项准确复现这一警告。","- 选项C（错误）：过度推理陷阱。AI scribe 负责记录和总结，原文没有说它替代医生临床判断。","- 选项D（错误）：细节偷换陷阱。口音问题是后文 AI receptionist 的另一个问题，不是首段 AI scribe 的唯一失败情形。","【命题技巧】","- 警告句主干定位：put them at risk by getting...wrong 是首段核心因果关系。","- 技术对象区分：AI scribe 与 AI receptionist 是两类应用，不能混同。"]},{"number":22,"stem":"Which of the following is true about the AI-scribe mistakes described in Paragraphs 2 to 6?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Patients may notice errors that health professionals miss, and uncorrected inaccuracies can remain in records"},{"key":"B","text":"The hospital corrected the woman's MRI result before she became aware of the mistake"},{"key":"C","text":"The migraine summary omitted a prescription instruction but a doctor discovered it in time"},{"key":"D","text":"Healthwatch found that clinicians were more likely than patients to catch transcription errors"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"[A]","analysis":["【题型】细节理解题·正误判断","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraphs 2-6","【解析】","- 原文（Para.2）：It was only when the patient, an NHS health professional, queried the AI tool's record of the result of her MRI scan that the hospital corrected it...","- 原文（Para.4）：...a blunder which, again, the patient, not the doctor, identified.","- 原文（Para.5）：...did not say that the hospital consultant had told the patient to seek a repeat prescription from their GP for their migraine...","- 原文（Para.6）：Healthwatch...has heard “multiple stories from patients who have noticed these errors when a health professional hasn't”...“These inaccuracies may persist in records if the patient doesn't catch them.”","分析：","- 选项A（正确）：多个案例显示错误常由患者而非医护发现；Healthwatch 明确指出，若患者没有发现，不准确信息可能持续留存在病历中。A 项完整概括两个风险。","- 选项B（错误）：时态与因果错位陷阱。原文说 It was only when the patient queried，说明医院是在患者质疑后才更正。","- 选项C（错误）：无中生有陷阱。原文说明摘要遗漏了重复处方指示，可能影响用药，但没有说医生及时发现。","- 选项D（错误）：反义干扰陷阱。原文反复强调 patients...when a health professional hasn't，说明 clinicians 更容易漏查，不是更容易发现。","【命题技巧】","- 跨案例归纳：MRI、药名混淆和处方遗漏共同指向患者纠错机制。","- 主体归属：patient 与 doctor/health professional 的对照是本题核心。"]},{"number":23,"stem":"It can be inferred from Paragraphs 7 to 10 that Healthwatch is especially concerned that _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"AI scribes have already eliminated most administrative errors in the NHS"},{"key":"B","text":"the NHS plans to stop using digital tools in its ten-year health plan"},{"key":"C","text":"AI scribes are being widely used without England-wide safety oversight"},{"key":"D","text":"ministers have prevented patients from suing doctors over AI mistakes"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"[C]","analysis":["【题型】推理判断题·监管缺口推理","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraphs 7-10","【解析】","- 原文（Para.7）：The government's 10-year health plan for the NHS in England expects AI scribes to “liberate staff from their current burden of bureaucracy...”","- 原文（Para.8）：...the urgent need for clarity over how patients can report and get corrected any mistakes made by AI scribing tools...","- 原文（Para.9）：...it is “worrying” that...MHRA has decided not to classify AI scribes as medical devices, which means there will be no England-wide oversight to ensure they are safe to use and effective. GPs and hospital doctors in England are already using 27 different AI scribes.","- 原文（Para.10）：Ministers have been warned that the NHS and medics could be sued over mistakes made by AI.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：反义干扰陷阱。Healthwatch 强调错误和更正机制不清，不是说行政错误大多已消除。","- 选项B（错误）：反义干扰陷阱。十年计划正推动 NHS 从 analogue 转向 digital-based service，不是停止数字工具。","- 选项C（正确）：MHRA 不将 AI scribes 归类为医疗器械，意味着没有英格兰范围内的监管来确保安全和有效；而医护已经在使用 27 种不同工具。这构成“广泛使用但缺统一监管”的核心担忧。","- 选项D（错误）：细节反转陷阱。部长们被告知 NHS 和医生可能因 AI 错误被起诉，不是阻止患者起诉。","【命题技巧】","- 监管与使用并置：no oversight 与 already using 27 tools 形成隐含风险。","- 政策目标与现实风险分层：效率目标不能自动消除安全性问题。"]},{"number":24,"stem":"Dr Shier Ziser Dawood would most likely agree that AI scribes _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"are ready to save GPs time because transcripts need no further checking"},{"key":"B","text":"should be banned because they deliberately prescribe dangerous drugs"},{"key":"C","text":"always invent medical instructions that patients immediately correct"},{"key":"D","text":"may create extra checking work that offsets their promised time savings"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"[D]","analysis":["【题型】推理判断题·人物观点归属","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraphs 12-13","【解析】","- 原文（Para.12）：Dr Shier Ziser Dawood...warned...that AI scribes may prove “a double-edged sword” for family doctors. She recounted how an AI scribe said she had told her patient to “continue their Prozac” even though she had not prescribed or discussed that drug.","- 原文（Para.13）：She warned that the need for doctors to review all transcripts in order to check for errors meant that AI tools are not yet proving time-saving.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：反义干扰陷阱。她强调医生必须 review all transcripts，且这些工具 not yet proving time-saving。","- 选项B（错误）：过度推理陷阱。AI scribe 记录了未发生的用药指示，但它不是医生，也没有主动开药；她未呼吁全面禁用。","- 选项C（错误）：绝对化陷阱。一个 Prozac 例子是 recounted case，不是所有记录都会被患者立即纠正。","- 选项D（正确）：她所说的 double-edged sword 正是：工具可能减轻文书负担，但为查错而审查全部记录又会增加工作，抵消省时收益。","【命题技巧】","- 人物观点边界：double-edged sword 要求同时承认潜在收益与成本。","- 证据强度控制：may/not yet 与正确项的委婉语气一致。"]},{"number":25,"stem":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Digital NHS Complete: How AI Scribes Have Made Medicine Error-Free"},{"key":"B","text":"AI Scribes in the NHS: Efficiency Promises, Safety Gaps and Patient Vigilance"},{"key":"C","text":"Typo or Diagnosis: Why Patients Should Never Read Medical Records"},{"key":"D","text":"Yorkshire Accents: The Greatest Barrier to Artificial Intelligence in England"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"[B]","analysis":["【题型】主旨大意题·最佳标题","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Full text","【解析】","- 原文（Para.7）：...AI scribes to “liberate staff from their current burden of bureaucracy...”","- 原文（Para.8）：...the urgent need for clarity over how patients can report and get corrected any mistakes made by AI scribing tools...","- 原文（Para.9）：...there will be no England-wide oversight to ensure they are safe to use and effective.","- 原文（Para.13）：...AI tools are not yet proving time-saving.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：反义干扰陷阱。文章显示错误、监管缺口和更正困难仍然存在，不是无错。","- 选项B（正确）：全文从 AI scribe 的政策承诺写起，列举误诊、药名和处方遗漏案例，再讨论监管缺位和医生复核成本。B 项同时覆盖效率承诺、安全缺口与患者需要警觉。","- 选项C（错误）：过度推理陷阱。患者发现问题反而促成更正，文章不是主张患者不读病历。","- 选项D（错误）：以偏概全陷阱。口音识别只是 AI receptionist 的一例，不是全文最大主题。","【命题技巧】","- 三层主旨整合：政策愿景、具体错误、监管与时间成本构成完整框架。","- 个案功能判断：demyelination、Prozac、Yorkshire accents 均服务更大的安全讨论。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语阅读理解大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。","如果对答案解析有疑惑，直接打开任何AI大模型（如Deepseek、Kimi等），上传该篇原文文档，然后复制粘贴对应的题目和选项，说清楚你的疑问，大模型会回应解释对应的问题。"]}]},{"file":"【福利】9.7检验题__完型填空(1).docx","type":"cloze","instructions":["Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)"],"passage":["In one case a woman was shocked when the AI scribe’s summary of her conversation wrongly said she had demyelination – serious nerve damage that can lead to multiple sclerosis. It was only when the patient, an NHS health professional, ____【11】____ the AI tool’s record of the result of her MRI scan that the hospital ____【12】____ it to what it should have been – “null demyelination”. “This was eventually corrected but it was a very ____【13】____ experience to be given an incorrect diagnosis because of AI and then be told it’s a typo,” said the woman, who asked not to be named.","Healthwatch England has ____【14】____ the case and other mistakes by AI scribes to show that the technology, which the NHS is introducing, is a potential ____【15】____ to patients’ safety. Unless ____【16】____, errors could end up in patients’ medical records and affect their ____【17】____, it said.","In another case an AI scribe ____【18】____ the drug the GP had prescribed with a different one of a similar name – a blunder which, again, the patient, not the doctor, ____【19】____.","On another occasion an AI-generated summary letter did not ____【20】____ that the hospital consultant had told the patient to seek a repeat prescription from their GP for their migraine, which could have left them unable to get their medication."],"items":[{"number":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"accepted"},{"key":"B","text":"queried"},{"key":"C","text":"copied"},{"key":"D","text":"ignored"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"B (queried)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·语境语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第2句","【解析】","[A] accepted（接受）：接受错误记录就无法触发后文医院更正。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] queried（质疑、询问）：queried the AI tool’s record 表示患者对 AI 记录提出疑问。正确答案。","[C] copied（复制）：复制记录不能发现错误。语义场干扰。","[D] ignored（忽视）：与后发现错误的方向相反。逻辑方向干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"deleted"},{"key":"B","text":"delayed"},{"key":"C","text":"corrected"},{"key":"D","text":"published"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (corrected)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·结果动作","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第2句","【解析】","[A] deleted（删除）：医院不是删除记录，而是改为正确结果。语义场干扰。","[B] delayed（延误）：未涉及时间延误。语义场干扰。","[C] corrected（更正）：corrected it to what it should have been 表示把它改成应有内容。正确答案。","[D] published（发布）：发布会扩大错误影响。逻辑方向干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"traumatising"},{"key":"B","text":"rewarding"},{"key":"C","text":"confusing"},{"key":"D","text":"boring"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"A (traumatising)","analysis":["【题型】形容词辨析·情感色彩","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第3句","【解析】","[A] traumatising（令人痛苦的）：被 AI 误诊为严重神经损伤，患者称之为 traumatising experience。正确答案。","[B] rewarding（有益的）：感情色彩相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[C] confusing（令人困惑的）：局部可通，但程度不足，不能体现误诊带来的心理冲击。程度差异干扰。","[D] boring（无聊的）：语义过轻。程度差异干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"hidden"},{"key":"B","text":"exaggerated"},{"key":"C","text":"highlighted"},{"key":"D","text":"dismissed"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (highlighted)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·报告行为","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第1句","【解析】","[A] hidden（隐藏）：患者权益机构是要公开案例。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] exaggerated（夸大）：文段列举真实错误，不是夸大。感情色彩干扰。","[C] highlighted（强调、引起注意）：highlighted the case 表示突出展示该案例。正确答案。","[D] dismissed（不予理会）：与警示目的相反。逻辑方向干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"benefit"},{"key":"B","text":"threat"},{"key":"C","text":"substitute"},{"key":"D","text":"barrier"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"B (threat)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·风险语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第1句","【解析】","[A] benefit（益处）：与错误和风险语境相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] threat（威胁）：a potential threat to patients’ safety 表示对病人安全的潜在威胁。正确答案。","[C] substitute（替代品）：文中未说 AI 取代现有系统。语义场干扰。","[D] barrier（障碍）：可指推广阻力，但此处是安全风险。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"ignored"},{"key":"B","text":"corrected"},{"key":"C","text":"deleted"},{"key":"D","text":"detected"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"D (detected)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·条件关系","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第2句","【解析】","[A] ignored（忽视）：Unless ignored 会让错误更可能进入记录，逻辑不合。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] corrected（更正）：语义接近，但 errors could end up 的前提是未被及时发现。近义干扰。","[C] deleted（删除）：删除错误不是此句条件重点。语义场干扰。","[D] detected（发现）：Unless detected 表示除非被发现，否则错误可能留在病历中。正确答案。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"care"},{"key":"B","text":"careers"},{"key":"C","text":"privacy"},{"key":"D","text":"reputation"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"A (care)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·医疗语境","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第2句","【解析】","[A] care（治疗、照护）：errors... affect their care 指错误影响患者后续治疗。正确答案。","[B] careers（职业）：患者个人治疗场景不是职业发展。形近干扰。","[C] privacy（隐私）：文中主要风险是诊疗错误，不是隐私泄露。语义场干扰。","[D] reputation（名声）：语义偏离。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"replaced"},{"key":"B","text":"translated"},{"key":"C","text":"compared"},{"key":"D","text":"confused"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"D (confused)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·固定搭配","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3, 第1句","【解析】","[A] replaced（替换）：replace A with B 是替换，而文中是混淆两个药名。近义干扰。","[B] translated（翻译）：不涉及语言转换。语义场干扰。","[C] compared（比较）：compared... with 可通，但未体现错误识别。近义干扰。","[D] confused（混淆）：confused the drug... with a different one 表示把一种药误作另一种药。正确答案。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"overlooked"},{"key":"B","text":"denied"},{"key":"C","text":"identified"},{"key":"D","text":"corrected"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (identified)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·主语行为","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3, 第1句","【解析】","[A] overlooked（忽视）：与错误被发现相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] denied（否认）：患者不是否认错误，而是指出错误。语义场干扰。","[C] identified（识别、发现）：the patient, not the doctor, identified 强调患者而非医生发现错误。正确答案。","[D] corrected（更正）：发现错误不等于负责修改记录。近义干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"deny"},{"key":"B","text":"say"},{"key":"C","text":"prove"},{"key":"D","text":"question"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"B (say)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·信息缺失","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 4, 第1句","【解析】","[A] deny（否认）：信件不是否认医嘱，而是遗漏医嘱。语义场干扰。","[B] say（说明、写道）：did not say that... 表示信中未说明医生曾告知患者复开处方。正确答案。","[C] prove（证明）：信息缺失不涉及证明。语义场干扰。","[D] question（质疑）：信件没有质疑医嘱。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语完型填空大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。"]}]}],"qa":[{"file":"9.7 答疑汇总(1).docx","items":[{"locator":"","sentence":"Dr Charlotte Blease, an expert in AI use in healthcare at Uppsala university in Sweden, said “AI can and does make mistakes”, but added: “The fact is, doctors can and do make mistakes without AI. And it is certainly possible the error rate is worse.”","question":"“当然，AI工具的出错率完全有可能更高。”最后一句不太理解为什么是ai工具，而不是医生。","answer":"本段围绕医疗AI笔录工具的风险展开讨论。专家先作出让步，承认即便不使用AI，医生本身也会出现失误，这并非在批判医生，而是将人工诊疗的出错情况作为参照基准。the error rate is worse不能理解为“医生本人的出错率变得更高”，原文没有任何信息表明AI会造成医生自身诊疗水平下降。译文将其解读为AI工具的出错率，是贴合整篇报道立场的处理方式。 严谨来看，the error rate is worse并不局限于AI工具本身的出错率，也可以指引入AI之后，整套诊疗流程的整体出错率，即AI产生错误再叠加人工核查疏漏，最终使得整体出错状况比纯人工模式下更差。原句存在成分省略，并未明确界定出错率的归属，本身带有一定语义模糊。结合全文大量实例：AI转录频频出错，且很多错误依靠患者才得以发现，文章意在突出AI带来的新增风险，因此中文译文处理为“AI工具的出错率完全有可能更高”。"}]}],"originals":[{"kind":"source","label":"原文","file":"9.7—9.13 原文(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":155662,"date":"2026-09-07","range":"2026-09-07|2026-09-13","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.7—9.13 原文(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"source","label":"原文","file":"9.7—9.13 原文(1).pdf","ext":".pdf","bytes":394514,"date":"2026-09-07","range":"2026-09-07|2026-09-13","title":"AI transcriptions of talks with doctors","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.7—9.13 原文(1).pdf","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"reading","label":"精读","file":"9.7 精读(1).pdf","ext":".pdf","bytes":847511,"date":"2026-09-07","range":"","title":"AI transcriptions of talks with doctors are","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.7 精读(1).pdf","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.7 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":55507,"date":"2026-09-07","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.7 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.7检验题__完型填空(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":59791,"date":"2026-09-07","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.7检验题__完型填空(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"qa","label":"答疑","file":"9.7 答疑汇总(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":20025,"date":"2026-09-07","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.7 答疑汇总(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"}]};
